      "commit": {
        "parents": [
          {
            "commit": "37d64682ef81cb8f304210f46d52dffb6c89ecbf",
            "subject": "LU-21328 efalnd: Undo a partial Tx mapping on failure"
          }
        ],
        "author": {
          "name": "Yonatan Nachum",
          "email": "ynachum@amazon.com",
          "date": "2026-11-15 08:09:10.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Yonatan Nachum",
          "email": "ynachum@amazon.com",
          "date": "2026-11-25 16:19:10.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-21328 efalnd: Don't use stale rdma desc for 0 size transfers",
        "message": "LU-21328 efalnd: Don't use stale rdma desc for 0 size transfers\n\nEach side of an RDMA transfer registers its buffer and keeps its\naddress, key and length in the Tx's RDMA descriptor. Txs are reused\nfrom a pool, and the descriptor is only written when a buffer is\nregistered, so until then it holds the previous transfer's values.\n\nA transfer with no data registers nothing and so uses those old\nvalues. The receiver of a PUT that LNet truncated to zero bytes still\nposts a read into the old buffer, and the source of a GET with an\nempty reply sends the old descriptor in its ack for the initiator to\nread from. The old key usually fails the read, leaving the peer\nwaiting for a completion that never comes. Once the key is reused,\nthe read can instead move another transfer's data.\n\nWhen the receiver of a PUT has no data, complete the PUT right away\ninstead of reading. When the source of a GET has no data, send an\nempty descriptor, and have the initiator complete the GET without\nreading, since a read from an empty descriptor fails.\n\nAlso reuse the Tx for the PUTR_DONE or NACK sent when mapping the\nPUT sink or GET source fails, instead of allocating a second Tx that\nmay not be available.\n\nAn initiator without this patch still reads from an empty\ndescriptor, but no in-tree LNet user sends a GET that can match an\nempty buffer: only the ping buffer allows truncation, and it is never\nempty.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nFixes: 0c773016d837 (\"LU-18977 efalnd: Introduce AWS EFA Lustre network driver\")\nSigned-off-by: Yonatan Nachum <ynachum@amazon.com>\nChange-Id: Id59971f1a070adf656a1c9124f876a53d76ebb39\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
